  "raw_text": "Step 1: To solve the equation \\( |x+5| - |3x-6| = 0 \\), we first recognize that this can be rewritten as \\( |x+5| = |3x-6| \\).\n\nStep 2: The equation \\( |x+5| = |3x-6| \\) implies two cases due to the definition of absolute values:\n- Case 1: \\( x+5 = 3x-6 \\)\n- Case 2: \\( x+5 = -(3x-6) \\)\n\nStep 3: Solve Case 1: \\( x+5 = 3x-6 \\)\n\\[ x + 5 = 3x - 6 \\]\n\\[ 5 + 6 = 3x - x \\]\n\\[ 11 = 2x \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{11}{2} \\]\n\nStep 4: Solve Case 2: \\( x+5 = -(3x-6) \\)\n\\[ x + 5 = -3x + 6 \\]\n\\[ x + 3x = 6 - 5 \\]\n\\[ 4x = 1 \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{1}{4} \\]\n\nStep 5: We have found two potential solutions: \\( x = \\frac{11}{2} \\) and \\( x = \\frac{1}{4} \\). The largest possible value of \\( x \\) is \\( \\frac{11}{2} \\).\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{\\frac{11}{2}}<|im_end|>",
